ולדותם", :translit "ya'akov hekidim dan l'gad k'seder tol'dotam", :gloss "Jacob placed Dan before Gad according to the order of their birth"} {:he "משה הקדים גד לדן לפי שהוא קבור בחלקו", :translit "moshe hekidim gad l'dan l'fi shehu k'bur b'chelko", :gloss "Moses placed Gad before Dan because he is buried in his portion"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "ולערב יחלק שלל", :b "הם הקרבנות"} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "בכורי אחיך אתה לחוף חמור ידין גדוד שמנה אילה בן זאב עולה", :spells "שם\"ה"} {:kind :other, :note "Sum שמ\"ה = 345, taken as solar-year day-count"} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "עלה בישראל מחלב צידון עובד יהיה עקב מלך שפר אחיו שלל עולה", :spells "שנ\"ד"} {:kind :other, :note "Sum שנ\"ד = 354, taken as lunar-year day-count"}], :frame {:text "Jacob's blessings to his sons in Genesis 49 follow the birth order for Dan (son of Bilhah, Gen 30:6) and Gad (son of Zilpah, Gen 30:11), so Dan precedes Gad. Moses's blessing of the tribes in Deuteronomy 33 places Gad before Dan; Ba'al HaTurim explains this reordering by Moses's burial in the portion of Gad (Deut 34:5–6, described as the land of Moab opposite Beth-peor). The solar year is counted as 365 days and the lunar year as 354 days in rabbinic calendrical calculation (see, e.g., the discussion of שנה מעוברת and the difference between sun and moon reckoning in b. Eruvin 56a, and Rashi ad loc. who cites 365 for the sun and 354–355 for the moon). Jeremiah 31:35–36 (31:34–35 in Hebrew) invokes the fixed ordinances of sun, moon, and stars as a guarantee of Israel's enduring nationhood.", :sources ["Genesis 30:6" "Genesis 30:11" "Deuteronomy 33:20-21" "Deuteronomy 34:5-6" "b. Eruvin 56a" "Rashi on b. Eruvin 56a" "Jeremiah 31:35-36"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "ולערב יחלק שלל", :a-value 816, :b "הם הקרבנות", :b-value 808, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "בכורי אחיך אתה לחוף חמור ידין גדוד שמנה אילה בן זאב עולה", :spells "שם\"ה", :letters ["ב" "א" "א" "ל" "ח" "י